# Set the expressive body with multi-image cross-frame reasoning fully clarified
body = [
    "💔The SwiftFox architecture has gone through SO many iterations and existential crises (: and no, today I'm not here to debate names 😭🙏.",
    "Tbh, it was lowkey such a fun and crazy project to build, and I'm definitely going to keep developing it. If you remember my October 28 post, I attached an image to my earlier prototype, asked it what was inside, and it literally hallucinated complete non-sense code with a perplexity of 850.309159... like, what else could I even expect?! 💔 That kind of pain makes you want to rewrite everything from absolute scratch.",
    "So I sat down and built SwiftFox: a multimodal language model implemented completely in PyTorch that ingests both image patches and raw text tokens and autoregressively generates clean responses. The architecture is built to work seamlessly whether you give it pure **Text → Text** or multiple visual inputs for **Image + Text → Text**.",
    "And before anyone asks: YES, it already has **native multi-image cross-frame reasoning** built right into its core! In my training pipeline, I pass up to 3 images at once (and structurally it can scale to even more). It doesn't process images in isolation — it flattens patches from ALL images into the exact same self-attention sequence, meaning the model can literally compare frames, track visual changes, and solve tasks like Spot-The-Diff natively 😭🙏.",
    "call:SwiftFox flattens all patches across multiple images and text tokens into a single autoregressive sequence, calculating dense cross-image attention with RoPE, and routing tokens through an Adaptive Entropy Mixture of Experts (MoE) powered by SwiGLU.",
    "diagram:swiftfox_flow",
    "## 1. The Development Timeline",
    "timeline:Oct 2026 | The 850 Perplexity Crisis | Feeding images into raw linear heads produced pure gibberish and broken code. Decided to nuke the old setup and start from scratch. | V1 FAIL\nOct 2026 | Multi-Image Patching & Residual 2D | Built 2D Residual Blocks (1x1 conv shortcuts + 3x3 convolutions) slicing each image into 16 patches of 64x64, supporting 5D batches (B, num_imgs, C, H, W). | VISION\nOct 2026 | Cross-Image Self-Attention & RoPE | Embedded patches from multiple frames into one unified sequence with RoPE, allowing patches from Image 1 and Image 2 to directly attend to each other. | ATTENTION\nOct 2026 | SwiGLU Gated Feedforward | Borrowed gated bilinear SwiGLU units from modern LLM architectures (like LLaMA and Ollama) for vastly superior representation stability. | EXPERTS\nOct 2026 | Adaptive Entropy MoE | Implemented dynamic Top-K routing based on normalized Shannon entropy H(x) so simple tokens don't waste 64 experts. | ROUTING\nOct 2026 | Load Balancing Loss & Cosine Schedule | Added auxiliary load balance loss (E * sum(P_i * f_i)) to stop router collapse and trained with Cosine Annealing. | STABLE",
    "## 2. Multi-Image Unified Sequence: How Cross-Frame Reasoning Actually Works",
    "Let's clear this up right away because this is one of the coolest parts of the whole model: how does SwiftFox look at multiple images and understand relationships between them? (: In my notebook (`very_very_random_file.ipynb`), I mapped out the exact sequence design:",
    "code:python:sequence_concept.py\n# The full multimodal sequence architecture:\n#\n# Pure text chat mode:\n# [<user>] {input} [<assistant>] {generated_text}\n#\n# Multi-Image mode (up to 3 images during training, expandable to more):\n# [IMG1_P1..IMG1_P16] [IMG2_P1..IMG2_P16] ... [IMG_K_P16] [<user>] [T1]..[TN] [<assistant>] [G1]..[GM]\n#\n# Each image is carved into 16 patches of 64x64.\n# When you pass 2 or 3 images, the transformer builds dense self-attention\n# across ALL patches of ALL images simultaneously!\n# Patch 4 of Image 1 directly attends to Patch 12 of Image 2!",
    "Think about what this means for cross-frame reasoning: most architectures force you to run separate image encoders, pool each image into a single vector, or use clunky cross-attention fusion adapters that lose spatial granularity. In SwiftFox, every image is sliced into 16 distinct patches (64x64). If you pass 2 images, you get 32 visual patch tokens. If you pass 3 images, you get 48 visual patch tokens.",
    "Because all 48 visual tokens are concatenated alongside the text prompt into the **same** Multi-Head Attention block, the transformer learns relationships between image patches *across different frames*! Image 2's patches can directly query Image 1's patches. That's how SwiftFox tackles datasets like Spot-the-diff and multi-image instructions: the model literally sees what changed from frame A to frame B.",
    "Here is the exact visual encoder implementation from my notebook handling 5D tensors `(B, num_imgs, C, H, W)` and slicing them into 16 spatial patches:",
    "code:python:image_encoder.py\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass ResidualBlock2D(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, in_, out_, stride_=1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.conv1 = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Conv2d(in_, out_, kernel_size=3, stride=stride_, padding=1),\n            nn.BatchNorm2d(out_),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Conv2d(in_, out_, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1),\n            nn.BatchNorm2d(out_)\n        )\n        self.identity_ = nn.Identity()\n        if in_ != out_ or stride_ != 1:\n            self.identity_ = nn.Sequential(\n                nn.Conv2d(in_, out_, kernel_size=1, stride=stride_),\n                nn.BatchNorm2d(out_)\n            )\n        self.relu = nn.ReLU()\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.relu(self.conv1(x) + self.identity_(x))\n\nclass ImageEncoder(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.more_layers = nn.Sequential(\n            ResidualBlock2D(3, 64),\n            ResidualBlock2D(64, 128),\n            ResidualBlock2D(128, 256),\n            nn.Conv2d(256, 256, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1),\n            nn.BatchNorm2d(256),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.AdaptiveMaxPool2d((8, 8)),\n            nn.Flatten(),\n            nn.Linear(256 * 8 * 8, d_model)\n        )\n\n    def forward(self, x, batch_size=None):\n        # Handles 5D tensor: (B, num_imgs, C, H, W)\n        if x.dim() == 5:\n            batch_size = x.size(0)\n            x = x.view(-1, x.size(2), x.size(3), x.size(4))\n        if batch_size is None:\n            batch_size = x.size(0)\n\n        total_images = x.size(0)\n        H_patch = x.size(2) // 4\n        W_patch = x.size(3) // 4\n        # Carve each image into 16 spatial patches\n        x = x.reshape(total_images, 16, x.size(1), H_patch, W_patch)\n        x = x.reshape(16 * total_images, x.size(1), H_patch, W_patch)\n        x = self.more_layers(x)\n        \n        # Returns (B, num_imgs * 16, d_model) ready to concatenate with text!\n        return x.reshape(batch_size, -1, x.size(-1))",
    "## 3. Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) & Attention",
    "Standard absolute positional encodings get super awkward when you glue multiple images in front of text tokens. Static 1D sinusoidal position vectors have no clue how to handle spatial patches, let alone multiple sequential images.",
    "Instead, I implemented **RoPE (Rotary Position Embeddings)** directly on Query and Key vectors. RoPE rotates pairs of coordinates in feature space using complex exponentials. When Query and Key compute dot-product attention, their absolute positions cancel out, leaving only the relative distance `(m - n)`! This makes relative spatial proximity between patches and relative causal distance between tokens feel completely natural.",
    "Here is the exact RoPE and Multi-Head Attention module from Cell 3 of `very_very_random_file.ipynb`:",
    "code:python:rope_mha.py\nimport math\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass RoPE(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, head_dim):\n        super().__init__()\n        inv_freq = torch.exp(\n            torch.arange(0, head_dim, 2) * (-1) * math.log(10000) / head_dim\n        )\n        self.register_buffer(\"inv_freq\", inv_freq)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        B, T, H, D = x.shape\n        position = torch.arange(0, T, device=x.device)\n        freq = torch.outer(position, self.inv_freq)\n\n        cos_ = freq.cos().unsqueeze(0).unsqueeze(0)\n        sin_ = freq.sin().unsqueeze(0).unsqueeze(0)\n\n        x_even = x[..., ::2]\n        x_odd = x[..., 1::2]\n        x_rot = x_even * cos_ - x_odd * sin_\n        y_rot = x_even * sin_ + x_odd * cos_\n        return torch.stack((x_rot, y_rot), dim=-1).flatten(-2)\n\nclass MHA(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model, nhead, dropout=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.d_model = d_model\n        self.nhead = nhead\n        self.head_dim = d_model // nhead\n\n        self.wq = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.wk = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.wv = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.wo = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.d1 = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n        self.d2 = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n        self.rope = RoPE(self.head_dim)\n\n    def forward(self, x, src_key_padding_mask=None, src_mask=None):\n        B, T, F = x.shape\n        k = self.wk(x).transpose(1, 2).view(B, T, self.nhead, self.head_dim)\n        q = self.wq(x).transpose(1, 2).view(B, T, self.nhead, self.head_dim)\n        v = self.wv(x).transpose(1, 2).view(B, T, self.nhead, self.head_dim)\n\n        k = self.rope(k)\n        q = self.rope(q)\n\n        score = (q @ k.transpose(-2, -1)) / math.sqrt(self.head_dim)\n        if src_key_padding_mask is not None:\n            src_key_padding_mask = src_key_padding_mask.unsqueeze(1).unsqueeze(2)\n            score = score.masked_fill(src_key_padding_mask == float(\"-inf\"), float(\"-inf\"))\n        if src_mask is not None:\n            score = score.masked_fill(src_mask, float(\"-inf\"))\n\n        score = torch.softmax(score, dim=-1)\n        score = self.d1(score)\n        score = score.view(B, T, F).contiguous()\n        return self.d2(self.wo(score))",
    "## 4. SwiGLU & The Adaptive Entropy MoE",
    "Now we get to the real meat of the architecture: the **Mixture of Experts (MoE)** backbone. Why run one monolithic feedforward layer when you can specialize computation across 64 specialized experts?",
    "### SwiGLU: Gated Feedforward",
    "First off, vanilla ReLU or GELU feedforward networks are cool, but modern LLMs get way better gradient flow and expressive capacity out of SwiGLU. The SwiGLU block splits the projection into a gated pair:",
    "code:python:swiglu.py\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass SwiGLU(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model, dim_feedforward):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.w1 = nn.Linear(d_model, dim_feedforward)\n        self.w2 = nn.Linear(d_model, dim_feedforward)\n        self.w3 = nn.Linear(dim_feedforward, d_model)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.w3(F.silu(self.w1(x)) * self.w2(x))",
    "### The Adaptive Top-K Router",
    "Here was my biggest realization: in standard MoE (like Mixtral 8x7B), K is strictly fixed to e.g. top-2. But why should every single token wake up the exact same number of experts?! 😭🙏",
    "If a token is dead obvious (like a period or a common preposition), the router distribution has high confidence, meaning Shannon entropy H(x) is almost 0. Waking up 4 or 8 heavy experts is just wasting GPU cycles! But when the token is tricky, ambiguous, or bridging visual and text features, the distribution flattens, entropy spikes, and you want to call in a whole squad of experts.",
    "So I formulated the **Adaptive Top-K rule**:",
    "formula:Adaptive Top-K Dynamic Allocation\nmath: K_{\\text{adaptive}} = K_{\\text{min}} + \\lfloor H(x) \\cdot (K_{\\text{max}} - K_{\\text{min}}) \\rfloor\ndesc: Scales expert capacity dynamically based on normalized Shannon routing entropy.\nvar: K_{\\text{min}} = Base floor of active experts (e.g. 4)\nvar: K_{\\text{max}} = Ceiling of active experts (e.g. 64)\nvar: H(x) = Normalized Shannon entropy: -sum(p * ln(p + 1e-9)) / ln(K_{\\text{max}})",
    "And here is the actual PyTorch implementation from Cell 10 of `very_very_random_file.ipynb`, routing experts dynamically and accumulating outputs with `output.index_put_`:",
    "code:python:adaptive_moe.py\nclass MoE(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, input_dim, hidden_dim, min_k=4, max_k=64):\n        super().__init__()\n        assert min_k < max_k\n        self.router = nn.Linear(input_dim, max_k)\n        self.experts = nn.ModuleList([\n            SwiGLU(input_dim, hidden_dim) for _ in range(max_k)\n        ])\n        self.max_k = max_k\n        self.min_k = min_k\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        B, T, F = x.shape\n        logits = self.router(x)\n        probs = torch.softmax(logits, dim=-1)\n        \n        # Normalized Shannon entropy\n        entropy = -torch.sum(probs * torch.log(probs + 1e-9), dim=-1) / math.log(self.max_k)\n        adaptive_k = self.min_k + torch.floor(entropy * (self.max_k - self.min_k)).long()\n\n        top_probs, top_indices = torch.topk(probs, adaptive_k, dim=-1)\n        weights = top_probs / top_probs.sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n\n        output = torch.zeros(B, T, F, device=x.device, dtype=x.dtype)\n\n        for i_exp, expert in enumerate(self.experts):\n            b_indices, t_indices, k_indices = torch.where(top_indices == i_exp)\n            if b_indices.numel() == 0:\n                continue\n            expert_weights = weights[b_indices, t_indices, k_indices]\n            expert_input = probs[b_indices, t_indices]\n            expert_output = expert(expert_input)\n\n            output.index_put_(\n                (b_indices, t_indices),\n                expert_weights * expert_output,\n                accumulate=True\n            )\n\n        return x + output, probs, top_indices",
    "## 5. Loss: Cross-Entropy + Load Balancing",
    "Anyone who has trained MoEs knows the infamous nightmare: router collapse 💔.",
    "If you don't incentivize the router to spread the work, it will quickly get lazy and send 99% of all tokens to Expert 0, while the remaining 63 experts sit around with zero gradients. To prevent this, I added an auxiliary **Load Balancing Loss** alongside the autoregressive cross-entropy loss:",
    "formula:Total SwiftFox Optimization Objective\nmath: \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{total}} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CE}} + \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LB}}\ndesc: Joint objective combining autoregressive token prediction with router distribution balance.\nvar: \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CE}} = Standard Cross Entropy loss computed on targets text[1:]\nvar: \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{LB}} = Load Balance Loss: E * sum(P_i * f_i)\nvar: P_i = Average routing probability across all tokens for expert i\nvar: f_i = Actual fraction of tokens assigned to expert i",
    "Here is the auxiliary load balance loss function directly from the notebook:",
    "code:python:load_balance.py\ndef load_balance_loss(probs, top_indices, n_e):\n    # probs: (B, T, E) routing probabilities\n    # top_indices: (B, T, K) assigned expert indices\n    B, T, F = probs.shape\n    importance = torch.mean(probs, dim=0) # average routing probability per expert\n    count = torch.bincount(top_indices.reshape(-1), minlength=n_e)\n    frequency = count.float() / (B * T * F) # frequency of assigned tokens\n    return n_e * torch.sum(importance * frequency)",
    "## 6. Autoregressive Prediction: Predicting text[1:]",
    "During training, the shifted sequence mechanics work cleanly:",
    "When I feed `model(text, images)`, what I pass into the network is the sequence without the last token. The model is trained to predict `text[1:]` (the tokens shifted by one step to the right). The loss is calculated exclusively on the generated assistant tokens, conditioning on both the preceding multi-image visual patches and the user instructions.",
    "code:python:training_step.py\n# Autoregressive forward pass with multiple images\nlogits, all_probs, all_indices = model(text=input_ids[:, :-1], images=images)\n\n# Main cross-entropy loss on next tokens text[1:]\ntargets = input_ids[:, 1:]\nloss_ce = criterion(logits.reshape(-1, logits.size(-1)), targets.reshape(-1))\n\n# Auxiliary MoE balance loss across all transformer layers\nloss_lb = 0.0\nfor probs, indices in zip(all_probs, all_indices):\n    loss_lb += load_balance_loss(probs, indices, n_e=64)\n\ntotal_loss = loss_ce + loss_lb\ntotal_loss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\nscheduler.step()",
    "💔Hope this is not a fail, whatever (: Watching the perplexity drop from that horrific 850.3 to crisp, coherent predictions on both multi-image inputs and text prompts makes the sleepless nights completely worth it.",
    "Having multi-image cross-frame reasoning natively working without heavy external vision backbones is super exciting. Next up: scaling up the parameter count, pushing the context window even further, and releasing the model checkpoints once fine-tuning wraps up. Stay tuned (: !"
]

